---
/**
 * ProductFlyout — the PRODUCTS mega-menu panel, from Figma `FlyoutMenu-product`
 * (`1017:3337`) on file Yjom8rPbyL5U1wtwOLkgtU. The hover state used to be a
 * second top-level frame, `1017:3029`; Ball deleted it on 2026-08-20 once the
 * rail became a component set with a `state` axis, so hover is a variant now
 * rather than a copy that could drift — and it had drifted, by an arrow.
 *
 * INTERNAL, for the same reason FooterProduct is: this is the CONTENT of a
 * panel, not a control anyone should reach for. `FlyoutMenu` owns the trigger,
 * the reveal and the ARIA; this owns what is inside. Keeping them apart is what
 * lets the panel be laid out and measured without a script running.
 *
 * Two scripts must know this folder exists, the same contract `field.ts` and
 * `FooterProduct.astro` already have: `build-utilities.mjs` compiles the classes
 * below and `check-component-vars.mjs` validates the variables they read. Miss
 * the first and the classes vanish from the stylesheet while the markup keeps
 * wearing them, with nothing red anywhere.
 *
 * ## The category header stopped being a deviation
 *
 * Until 2026-08-19 the label was a RAW `#678CB1` in the node — the only unbound
 * colour in it — and this file shipped `--color-info-strong` instead. Ball bound
 * it to `iron-blue/800` and put the group opacity on it in the same pass. This
 * file then read `opacity-75, where Figma says 70` for a day; re-audited
 * 2026-08-20, the node says 75 as well, so the two sides agree and there is
 * nothing left to justify. The numbers are at the header itself.
 *
 * ## Two places this deliberately does not copy Figma, all measured
 *
 * 1. **This component has ONE mode — Ball, 2026-08-19.** There is no dark design
 *    for the mega-menu and there is not going to be one, which is why the ten
 *    product accents and `iron-blue-800` are ramp values here rather than
 *    semantic tokens that remap: they are each product's identity colour and a
 *    dark counterpart would be a different brand.
 *
 *    The one loose end that follows, recorded rather than fixed: the SURFACES
 *    still use tokens that do remap — `bg-bg-card` becomes iron-violet/800 under
 *    `.dark`, so a consumer who wraps this in a dark scope gets a purple panel
 *    with light-mode contents. Nothing in this repo applies `.dark` to these
 *    pages, so it is latent rather than live. Pinning the surfaces is a design
 *    decision, not a cleanup, so it waits for one.
 *
 * 2. **The rail chrome is inlined rather than exported.** The rainbow IS artwork
 *    and stays an exported file — as the RAIL's OWN `background-image`, which is
 *    load-bearing and not a style preference. `npm run audit` decides whether to
 *    resolve a text run's backdrop from painted pixels by walking the run's
 *    ANCESTOR chain for a `background-image`; artwork on a sibling is invisible
 *    to it, so it composed the backdrop from background COLOURS and reported the
 *    1% label as `#E5E5E5 on #FFFFFF`. The pixels actually under those glyphs are
 *    `#B21A21`, `#7B57A7` and `#3986BD`. Moving it from a child `<img>` to a
 *    sibling `background-image` changed NOTHING — the reading only moved once the
 *    image landed on the rail itself. A backdrop that is never painted is worse
 *    than no reading at all: an exemption keyed to it would be keyed to a
 *    fiction. The pointed rail edge, the card's side notch and the
 *    panel arrow are white/border-coloured MASKS — an exported white shape can
 *    only ever be white, and this system has a full dark ladder. The three paths
 *    below are the exported vector data verbatim, re-fitted to `currentColor` so
 *    they follow the surface they sit on. Nothing here is hand-drawn.
 *
 * ## The rail scrim is a gradient, not a flat black
 *
 * Sampling the render says the rail darkens to ~53% of its lit value, which does
 * not fall out of a flat 40% black. The node is a `to bottom` gradient from
 * `black` to `rgba(132,66,124,0.8)`, `mix-blend-multiply`, at `opacity-40` —
 * `opacity-70` on hover. That is the whole hover change on this half, together
 * with the CTA moving to its own hover token, which `Button` already does.
 */
import Logo from '../components/Logo.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { icons } from '../icons';

/* The lockup is `weight/bold` 700 + `weight/medium` 500, and BOTH halves were
   wrong at first. The prefix used `font-h4`, borrowed from FooterProduct, which
   is 800 — a heading role, not this one. The suffix was `font-normal` 400 until
   Figma moved it to 500 on 2026-08-19. Verified in the browser across all 33
   lockups on the page rather than read off the source, because the first probe
   written for it matched the OUTER wrapper and reported every column shifted by
   one: the whole "IRONPDF" run as the prefix at 400, the inner "IRON" as the
   suffix at 800. Ask for the element whose text is exactly `IRON`. */

/** Which brand accent the coloured half of a product name wears. Figma binds
 *  exactly these four across the ten products — read off each `Product Info`
 *  node rather than sampled, since antialiased text lies about its own colour. */
export type FlyoutAccent = 'primary' | 'accent-1' | 'accent-2' | 'accent-3';

export interface FlyoutProduct {
  /** `Logo` kind="element" key — the 56px mark. */
  product: 'pdf' | 'word' | 'xl' | 'ppt' | 'ocr' | 'barcode' | 'qr' | 'zip' | 'print' | 'webscraper';
  /** Bold half. "IRON" throughout the design; a prop so it is not baked in. */
  prefix?: string;
  /** Coloured half, e.g. "PDF". */
  suffix: string;
  accent: FlyoutAccent;
  desc: string;
  href?: string;
}

export interface FlyoutCategory {
  /** Rendered uppercase by the type role, so pass it in sentence case. */
  label: string;
  /** Key into `icons`. Absent until the three Pro glyphs are outlined — see the
   *  note at the top of this file. */
  icon?: keyof typeof icons;
  products: FlyoutProduct[];
}

interface Props {
  categories: FlyoutCategory[];
  /** The left rail. Omit to render the columns alone. */
  suite?: {
    /** Bold half of the lockup wordmark. */
    prefix?: string;
    /** Coloured half. */
    suffix?: string;
    desc?: string;
    ctaLabel?: string;
    ctaHref?: string;
    /** Text beside the 1%-for-the-Planet mark. */
    donateLabel?: string;
  };
  /** Where `docs/assets/` was copied to — same escape hatch as `Logo`. */
  basePath?: string;
  /** Renders the rail and the first product in their hover state, for the docs
   *  page. `:hover` cannot be captured statically, and the two halves hover
   *  independently, so this forces both at once for one illustration. */
  forceHover?: boolean;
  class?: string;
}

const {
  categories,
  suite,
  basePath = 'assets',
  forceHover = false,
  class: className,
} = Astro.props;

const s = {
  prefix: 'IRON',
  suffix: 'SUITE',
  desc: 'All 10 .NET Libraries',
  ctaLabel: 'Save 80% with Iron Suite',
  ctaHref: '#',
  donateLabel: 'Every Iron Suite Donates',
  ...suite,
};

/* Figma binds the coloured half to one of four brand accents. Mapped here rather
   than passed as a CSS colour (which is what FooterProduct does) because this
   set is closed and naming it keeps `check:tokens` able to see it. */
const ACCENT: Record<FlyoutAccent, string> = {
  'primary':  'text-primary',
  'accent-1': 'text-accent-1',
  'accent-2': 'text-accent-2',
  'accent-3': 'text-accent-3',
};

/* Asset names are encoded, not interpolated raw — the same rule FooterBar and
   the README already carry. `1% logo - Horizontal.png` contains a percent sign
   and `% l` is an invalid escape sequence, so the browser fails the whole URL
   and renders a broken image. Interpolating it raw is exactly what this file did
   on its first run, and the render harness refused the request before a browser
   ever saw it. */
const asset = (file: string) => `${basePath}/${encodeURIComponent(file)}`;

/* Exported vector data, verbatim — see note 3 at the top. Each is mirrored in
   Figma by a transform on its wrapper rather than in the path, so the mirror
   stays in CSS here too and the `d` can be diffed against a fresh export. */
const RAIL_EDGE = 'M0 0H22L1.56627 182.664C1.16954 186.21 1.16954 189.79 1.56627 193.336L22 376H0V0Z';
const CARD_NOTCH = 'M11.7348 0L0.263738 108.975C-0.087913 112.316 -0.0879125 115.684 0.263739 119.025L11.7348 228V0Z';
---

{/* An ::after ring, not a border and not an outline. Figma's stroke is `Inside`,
    which costs the frame nothing there — the card is 1200x376 with the line
    painted within it.

    A real border cannot do that here: the panel's height comes from its content,
    so `box-sizing` has nothing to subtract from and 1px a side made it 378.
    An outline at `-1px` kept the box at 376 but only drew on three edges —
    outlines paint in the parent's own layer, and the rail is a CHILD with a
    background, so it covered the left edge. Both were measured before landing
    here. An absolutely-positioned ring paints above every child, adds no layout,
    and inherits the radius. */}
<div
  class:list={['pf relative flex items-stretch w-full max-w-[1200px] bg-bg-card rounded-xl shadow-modal overflow-hidden',
    'after:content-[""] after:pointer-events-none after:absolute after:inset-0 after:rounded-[inherit] after:border after:border-border',
    className]}
  data-part="panel"
>
  {suite && (
    <div
      class:list={[
        'group/rail relative flex flex-col items-start justify-center shrink-0 box-border w-[264px] pl-md',
        'bg-no-repeat [background-size:100%_100%]',
        /* 264 rail + 24 gutter + 282.67 for one column + 24 = 594.67 is the
           narrowest width that can hold the rail AND a column. Below it the
           rail goes rather than squashing the products it exists to sell. The
           number is the measurement, not a round breakpoint. */
        'max-[594.98px]:hidden',
        forceHover && 'is-hover',
      ]}
      style={`background-image: url(${asset('flyout-rail-rainbow.svg')})`}
      data-part="rail"
    >
      {/* The scrim over the rail's own artwork. `multiply` is what keeps the
          stripes reading as stripes instead of flattening them toward one grey. */}
      <div class="absolute inset-0 overflow-hidden" aria-hidden="true">
        <div
          class="absolute inset-0 mix-blend-multiply opacity-[0.4] transition-opacity duration-[var(--duration-base)] group-hover/rail:opacity-[0.7] group-focus-within/rail:opacity-[0.7] group-[.is-hover]/rail:opacity-[0.7]"
          style="background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--scrim-rail-from), var(--scrim-rail-to));"
        />
      </div>

      {/* The pointed right edge. White in Figma; `text-bg-card` here so it is the
          panel's own surface and follows dark mode. */}
      <div class="absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-[22px] text-bg-card scale-x-[-1]" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg class="block w-full h-full" viewBox="0 0 22 376" fill="currentColor" preserveAspectRatio="none">
          <path d={RAIL_EDGE} />
        </svg>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card relative z-[1] flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-md bg-bg-card rounded-lg px-sm py-md">
        <div class="flex flex-col items-center gap-sm w-full">
          <Logo kind="mark" product="suite" size={96} basePath={basePath} label="" />
          <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-xs pt-[6px] w-full">
            <p class="flex items-start text-xl leading-none tracking-h4 whitespace-nowrap m-0">
              <span class="font-bold text-text-heading">{s.prefix}</span><span class="font-medium text-primary">{s.suffix}</span>
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm leading-5 font-normal text-text-body text-center w-full m-0">{s.desc}</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {/* Figma's `product-suite` (`1039:2429`) has TWO states and the CTA moves
            WITH the rail in both: `State=hover` (`1039:2427`) is scrim 0.7 AND
            `button/primary-hover`. Until 2026-08-20 this line was bare, so the
            CTA only darkened when the pointer was on the BUTTON — hovering the
            rail anywhere else rendered scrim 0.7 with a resting button, a state
            the design does not contain. `forceHover` could not reach it at all:
            Button's hover is a real `:hover`, and a static docs page has none,
            so the page illustrating the hover state showed half of it.

            Passed through Button's OWN `class` prop rather than by reaching into
            it. The rule here is that a component publishes parts, never its
            classes — and `class` is published. It beats the variant's
            `bg-button-primary` on SPECIFICITY rather than order: the group
            variant compiles to `.group\/rail:hover .…` at (0,2,0) against
            (0,1,0). Verified in the browser, not assumed — the `wrap` note in
            Button records the case where a passed class was one deep, tied, and
            lost. */}
        <Button
          size="sm"
          variant="primary"
          href={s.ctaHref}
          class="group-hover/rail:bg-button-primary-hover group-focus-within/rail:bg-button-primary-hover group-[.is-hover]/rail:bg-button-primary-hover"
        >{s.ctaLabel}</Button>

        {/* The card's own notch, pointing into the columns. */}
        <div class="absolute right-[-12px] top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2 w-[12px] h-[228px] text-bg-card scale-x-[-1]" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="block w-full h-full" viewBox="0 0 11.7348 228" fill="currentColor" preserveAspectRatio="none">
            <path d={CARD_NOTCH} />
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="absolute bottom-md left-md z-[1] flex items-center gap-[6px] text-2xs leading-3 font-semibold tracking-[0.3px] text-text-on-dark-body whitespace-nowrap m-0">
        {s.donateLabel}
        <img class="block w-[56.287px] h-[24px]" src={asset("1% logo - Horizontal.png")} alt="1% for the Planet" />
      </p>
    </div>
  )}

  {/* The plain `grid` utility, which for one day it could not use: fifteen docs
      pages declared their own `.grid` for spec cards, in an inline <style> that
      outranks utilities.css, so the page won every column rule set here and
      three sweeps measured the page's 220px grid while reporting it as this
      one's. Those rules are `.spec-grid` now and `check:docs-css` asks Tailwind
      whether any page class would shadow a utility, so the trap cannot come back
      for the next component either.

      Measured breakpoints, not `auto-fit`. The panel is `min(1200, 100vw - 48)`
      wide and the columns get what is left after the 264 rail and 48 of padding,
      so three need a 1200 panel (viewport 1248), two need 897.34 (viewport
      945.34) and one needs 282.67. `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(282.67px,100%),
      1fr))` was tried first and swept across 24 widths: it went 3 -> 2 -> 1 -> 2
      -> 1 columns, fitting tracks below their own minimum at 768 and 1000, and
      leaking 1.2px out of the panel at 1000. `minmax(0, 1fr)` cannot overflow,
      and the two numbers above are the content's own, not round ones. */}
  <div class="pf-cols grid gap-lg grow min-w-0 pt-[32px] px-xl pb-xl [grid-template-columns:repeat(3,minmax(0,1fr))] max-[1247.98px]:[grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr))] max-[945.33px]:[grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr)]" data-part="columns">
    {categories.map((cat) => (
      <div class="pf-col flex flex-col gap-md min-w-0" data-part="column">
        {/* `opacity-75` on the GROUP, which carries the icon and the label
            together, so the label is what decides it. `iron-blue/800` is 9.27:1
            on its own; at 70 it composited to #5D809D and 4.17:1 on the panel,
            under the 4.5 bar 14px bold has to meet, so 75 was chosen and Figma
            followed. Both say 75 now. Ball's call, 2026-08-19.

            The governing number is the PANEL — 4.73:1 — because this header is a
            SIBLING of the product list and never sits inside a row, so the row
            hover fill is not its backdrop. `npm run audit` confirms it: this run
            is only ever reported on #FFFFFF. Recorded because the figure moved
            anyway when the hover fill changed on 2026-08-20 — the hypothetical
            composite over the fill went 4.54 (#F2F9FE) to 4.46 (#F1F5F9), below
            the bar. Nothing renders it. If this header is ever moved into a row,
            that is the number to re-derive first. */}
        <p class="flex items-center gap-xs pl-xs m-0 text-iron-blue-800 opacity-75">
          {cat.icon && (
            <svg class="w-[16px] h-[16px] shrink-0" viewBox={icons[cat.icon].viewBox} fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d={icons[cat.icon].path} />
            </svg>
          )}
          <span class="text-overline leading-overline font-overline tracking-overline uppercase">{cat.label}</span>
        </p>

        {/* `gap-xs` is the 8 the rows gained when Ball rebuilt the row as a
            component on 2026-08-24. The rows had abutted at 72 tall; at the
            ladder's 48 mark they are 64, and rather than closing the list up he
            kept the 72 PITCH and let the difference become separation. That is
            the right call for a row whose hover state is a `rounded-lg` fill —
            abutting pills read as one block — and it is why the panel is 368 and
            not the 344 I predicted from rows closing up. */}
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-xs list-none m-0 p-0">
          {cat.products.map((p, i) => (
            <li>
              <a
                class:list={[
                  /* Hover fill and radius are Figma's, re-read 2026-08-20: the row
                     carries `rounded-lg` 8px, and the fill moved from
                     `surface/card-alt` to `surface/background-shade` #F1F5F9,
                     which is `--color-bg-shade` hex for hex. Three `npm run
                     audit` exemptions are keyed to that backdrop and were
                     re-measured with it — see KNOWN in scripts/preview.mjs. */
                  'group/row flex items-center gap-sm px-micro py-xs rounded-lg no-underline text-inherit',
                  'transition-colors duration-[var(--duration-base)]',
                  'hover:bg-bg-shade focus-visible:bg-bg-shade',
                  'focus-visible:outline-[length:var(--border-width-2)] focus-visible:outline-solid focus-visible:outline-border-focus focus-visible:outline-offset-[-2px]',
                  forceHover && i === 0 && 'bg-bg-shade',
                ]}
                href={p.href ?? '#'}
                data-part="row"
              >
                {/* 48 is the ladder size nearest what this row used to carry.
                    It was 56 until 2026-08-24 — an off-ladder size that existed
                    only because this frame had one, and where all ten Figma
                    instances measured 56.000003814697266, i.e. dragged rather
                    than chosen. `Logo` writes width/height as an INLINE style,
                    so a class here could never override it; the size has to come
                    through the prop. See the `size` prop's note. */}
                <Logo kind="element" product={p.product} size={48} basePath={basePath} label="" class="shrink-0" />
                <span class="flex flex-col items-start gap-micro flex-1 min-w-0">
                  <span class="flex items-start text-lg leading-5 tracking-h4 whitespace-nowrap">
                    <span class="font-bold text-text-heading">{p.prefix ?? 'IRON'}</span><span class:list={['font-medium', ACCENT[p.accent]]}>{p.suffix}</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-xs leading-4 font-normal text-text-body min-w-0">{p.desc}</span>
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</div>
